TITLE - Getting There Was Harder Than Getting In

If you're reading this years from now, you'll probably remember Hong Kong.

You probably won't remember everything it took just to get there.

When I first got the opportunity to attend the conference, the biggest challenge wasn't the research paper.

It wasn't the visa.

It wasn't the flight.

It was convincing my parents to let me go.

Looking back, I completely understand why they were hesitant.

It was my first international trip.

I was still a college student.

And I was travelling to another country without my family.

It wasn't an easy "yes."

I still remember my dad coming to college to talk to one of my professors about the conference.

The funny part?

He accidentally walked into her cabin...

...while she was having lunch.

I can only imagine how awkward that must have been.

Somehow, despite all the awkwardness, the conversations, and the convincing...

they finally agreed.

Looking back now...

I'm incredibly grateful they did.

Because if they hadn't...

none of the stories you're about to read would have existed.


SUB :-Before the Flight

There are a lot of things I'll probably forget about this trip.

This conversation isn't one of them.

I still remember taking the airport bus to the airport with my dad.

I was scared and excited at the same point.

Not because I was flying.

Not because I was travelling abroad.

I was scared because it was my first international conference. My first time leaving India. My first time standing on a stage where I wasn't just another college student.

Dad noticed.
I still remember the drive to the airport.

I was nervous.

Not because of the flight.

Not because of Hong Kong.

I was nervous because this was my first time leaving India on my own.

Dad could tell.

For most of the drive, he spoke about random things.

Then, just before we reached the airport, he became serious.

He looked at me and said,

"Nothing is going to happen if you mess up."

"The world isn't going to fall apart."

Then came the part that still makes me smile.

"And stop saying 'fuck' every five minutes."

I had a terrible habit back then.

Whenever I got excited, surprised, frustrated, or even happy...

"fuck" somehow became punctuation.

Then he looked at me again and said,

"You're not representing your college anymore."

"You're representing your country."

That sentence hit differently.

I don't remember exactly what I replied.

But I do remember looking out of the car window and thinking,

Whatever happens over the next few days... I'm not going to let fear stop me from living this experience.



SUB---------------- Arrival--------------------
Landing in Hong Kong was probably the first time I truly realized that I was no longer in India.

Everything felt... compact.

The roads.

The buildings.

The traffic.

There were skyscrapers everywhere.

It almost felt like the city had no option but to grow upwards because there simply wasn't any land left.

As we drove through the city, I couldn't stop staring out of the window.

Every building looked like something straight out of a movie.

I remember thinking,

"This place is going to be very different."

I wasn't wrong.

SUB -------------------------- Chungking Mansions --------------------------
To save some money, I had booked a room in one of Hong Kong's most iconic buildings—Chungking Mansions.

If you've never heard of it before, neither had I.

Only after reaching Hong Kong did I realize the reputation the place had.

People online described it as everything from a backpacker's paradise to one of the sketchiest buildings in the city.

To make things even better...

I remember hearing that just a day before I checked in, a Hong Kong police officer had been murdered nearby.

Safe to say...

my confidence wasn't exactly increasing.

But after spending some time there, I slowly started understanding why so many people still stayed there.

It wasn't fancy.

It wasn't luxurious.

It was affordable.

Walking through the building felt like walking through a miniature version of the world.

Every few steps I heard a different language.

Hindi.

Urdu.

Bengali.

English.

Cantonese.

People from India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, different African countries, Vietnam...

everyone seemed to pass through Chungking Mansions at some point.

The corridors were crowded.

The elevators were tiny.

The rooms...

even tinier.

Surprisingly...

I never really felt uncomfortable.

Maybe four years in a boys' hostel had already prepared me for controlled chaos.

Compared to hostel life...

Chungking Mansions honestly wasn't that bad.

SUB ----------------------------Back at the Hotel ----------------------------
Later that evening, after returning from the conference with my new Thai friends, I squeezed into one of those tiny elevators.

When I say tiny...

I mean there was barely enough room for two people.

It was just me and a Chinese woman who looked like she was in her mid-twenties.

Completely by accident, I glanced at her phone.

She was texting someone about the hotel.

"It's really cheap... the room is okay... but the Indian staff are kind of creepy."

I immediately looked away and tried my best not to laugh.

A few seconds later, she quietly put her phone back into her pocket, looked at me, and asked,

"Excuse me... where's the exit?"

I pointed her in the right direction.

She thanked me.

I smiled.

We walked out of the building in opposite directions.

I'll probably never meet her again.

But for some reason...

that's one of those tiny moments from Hong Kong that has stayed with me.

SUB----------Meeting Boom

Before the conference officially began, we had to collect our conference kits the previous day.

That was also the day I met Boom.

I still remember waiting at the bus stop in what felt like the middle of rural Hong Kong.

There weren't many people around.

Then I noticed this guy.

To me, he looked Chinese.

Later I'd find out he was actually from Thailand.

He looked at me...

smiled...

and I remember thinking,

Okay... at least one person here doesn't think I'm completely lost.

Years later, I found out what was going through his head.

He told me,

"I saw an Indian guy standing at the bus stop, and I thought... he's definitely coming to the conference."

Meanwhile...

my brain was much simpler.

"The only Chinese-looking guy who smiled at me."

That one smile made the place feel a little less unfamiliar.

SUB ------------------------ Breakfast--------


That's when we actually started talking.

We ended up having Breakfast at the SChool Canteen/Mess.

There was one small problem.

I'd never tasted authentic soy noodles before.

One bite...

My taste buds immediately started negotiating with my stomach.

I was trying my absolute best not to offend anyone.

Everyone noticed.

Everyone laughed.

I laughed.

And somehow...

over a breakfast that my taste buds completely disagreed with...

we became friends.

SUB---------------------------The Morning of the Conference--------------------

The morning of the conference had finally arrived.

For once in my life...

I actually woke up on time.

I brushed my teeth, got my clothes ready, and jumped into the shower, thinking everything was going according to plan.

Well...

life had other plans.

I turned on the shower and quickly checked the water temperature with my hand.

It felt fine.

So naturally, I turned around and let the water hit my back.

Big mistake.

Somehow the temperature suddenly shot up.

Boiling hot water landed straight on my back.

I jumped out of the shower like I'd just stepped on a landmine.

For the next couple of minutes, I was lying on the bed, squirming around and wondering how I had managed to injure myself before the conference had even started.

Eventually, I gave up.

I took the quickest cold-water bath of my life, got dressed, grabbed my conference bag, and rushed out of the hotel.

By the time I reached the conference venue...

breakfast was already over.

Even worse...

the official group photo had already been taken.

My heart sank.

This was my first international conference, and somehow I had already managed to miss the one picture everyone would probably keep.

I found Jessica, one of the organizers, and nervously explained what had happened.

Instead of getting annoyed, everyone was incredibly understanding.

They reassured me that it was completely fine and helped me figure out where I needed to be.

That was the moment my nervousness started disappearing.

Until then, everyone had just been strangers.

But from that point onwards...

they became people who genuinely wanted me to have a good experience.


SUB--------------------Presentation-------------------

By the time my presentation started, I had somehow managed to survive the hot shower, missing breakfast, and rushing into the conference hall.

Now came the actual reason I had travelled all the way to Hong Kong.

Standing on that stage felt different.

This wasn't another classroom presentation.

This wasn't another college seminar.

People in that room had travelled from different countries to attend the conference.

For the first few minutes, everything went according to plan.

Then...

my mind went completely blank.

Not for long.

Just enough for me to realize...

"Well... this isn't ideal."

I paused.

Took a deep breath.

Looked at my slides again.

Collected my thoughts.

And continued.

Nobody laughed.

Nobody interrupted.

The world didn't fall apart.

Ironically...

Dad had been right.

"Nothing is going to happen if you mess up."

I finished the presentation, answered the questions that followed, and walked off the stage feeling relieved.

Looking back now, I don't even remember the questions people asked.

I remember something much more important.

I learned that it's perfectly okay to pause.

You don't have to fill every second with words.

Sometimes...

taking one deep breath is all it takes to get back on track.

SUB ----------------------The Cab Ride--------------------------------
After the conference ended, it was time to head back to the hotel.

By then, I had already made a small group of friends—Boom, Japan (yes, that was his nickname), and two teachers from Thailand.

Naturally, all of us wanted to travel together.

We had already picked our seats.

Everyone got into the cab.

Mission accomplished.

Or so we thought.

One of the conference volunteers suddenly realized that someone had to ride with another group to guide the driver to the hotel.

The cab door opened again.

Boom looked around.

Everyone looked at Boom.

Boom realized...

he was the chosen sacrifice.

He got out of the cab while the rest of us just watched.

The atmosphere was hilarious.

His Thai friends looked disappointed because they thought he'd be travelling with them.

Boom just looked at us and smiled.

"I'll see you guys back at the hotel."

And just like that...

our perfectly planned cab ride disappeared.

While all this was happening, I was helping Yuchen load his suitcase into the boot.

By the time I finished and got into the cab...

I realized I had somehow ended up sitting between Yuchen and a teacher from Nigeria.

I didn't mind.

But judging by the expression on my face...

everyone else could tell I was a little nervous.

The teacher looked at me, smiled, and said,

"You're the man of the hour. It's our responsibility to keep you warm."

I remember thinking,

"Well... that wasn't exactly the icebreaker I was expecting."

Everyone burst into laughter.

And somehow...

the awkwardness disappeared before the cab had even started moving.


SUB ---------------------- The Chinese Banquet ----------------------

A few minutes later, we finally reached the hotel.

I genuinely thought we'd have a quick dinner, chat for a bit, and head back to our rooms after an exhausting day.

I couldn't have been more wrong.

The conference organizers had arranged a traditional Chinese banquet for all the delegates.

The moment I walked into the dining hall, I just stood there for a second taking everything in.

Instead of small individual tables, there were huge round tables spread across the hall, each seating ten to twelve people.

In the middle of every table was a large rotating glass tray.

I had only seen something like that in movies.

Every few minutes, waiters would walk in carrying massive dishes and place them right in the centre of the table.

A whole fish.

Roasted duck.

Chicken.

Pork.

Vegetables.

Soup.

Everything was shared.

Whenever someone wanted something, they'd gently rotate the glass tray until the dish reached them, take what they wanted, and rotate it for the next person.

It was one of those tiny cultural differences that immediately stood out to me.

Back home, everyone usually has their own plate of food.

Here...

the entire meal felt like a shared experience.

Everyone around the table was talking.

People were introducing themselves.

Passing dishes to one another.

Making sure nobody missed out on anything.

It didn't feel like a formal conference dinner anymore.

It felt like a bunch of strangers slowly becoming friends over food.

SUB ---------------------- The Pork That Started It All ----------------------

Now before I tell you about the absolute disaster that followed...

I have to appreciate one thing.

The roasted pork.

To this day, I still think it was one of the best things I've ever eaten.

The skin was unbelievably crispy.

You could actually hear the crunch.

Then, as you bit further in, the meat became softer...

juicier...

and almost melted in your mouth.

I don't think I'd ever had pork cooked like that before.

Back in India, whenever my Muslim friends ask me about Hong Kong, I somehow always end up describing this roasted pork in painful detail.

I'm pretty sure I've made more than one of them regret asking.

Ironically...

out of everything served that evening...

that was probably the only dish my taste buds completely agreed with.

Everything else...

they were still trying to negotiate.

SUB ---------------------- Two Chopsticks... Why?

Just when I thought I had figured out how this dinner worked...

I looked down at my place setting.

There were bowls.

Tiny plates.

Tea cups.

A spoon.

And...

two pairs of chopsticks.

One pair was red.

The other was black.

I remember staring at them for a good few seconds.

"Why do I need two?"

Then I looked around the table.

To my surprise...

I wasn't the only confused one.

Even some of the delegates from mainland China were looking at the chopsticks with the exact same expression.

Apparently, they had never seen two differently coloured chopsticks at a banquet before either.

For a brief moment...

I felt strangely relieved.

"Finally... something even they don't know."

Naturally, someone called one of the waitresses over.

She smiled, pointed at the chopsticks, and explained that one pair was meant for serving food from the common dishes onto your own bowl, while the other pair was meant for actually eating.

Simple.

At least...

that's what everyone else thought.

SUB ---------------------- My First Time Holding Chopsticks

Now here's the catch.

I was probably the only Indian sitting at that table who had never held a pair of chopsticks before.

I'd seen them.

Anime.

Ninja Hattori.

Movies.

Restaurants.

But actually holding them...

that was a completely different experience.

Everyone else around me had grown up using chopsticks.

The delegates from Thailand had been eating with them their whole lives.

The Chinese delegates obviously knew exactly what they were doing.

And then there was me...

holding two wooden sticks like I was trying to solve a mechanical engineering problem.

At that moment, I suddenly remembered one of my friends back in India.

She had been obsessed with Japanese food for years.

She'd invited me countless times to come try authentic Japanese food with her.

Every single time...

I'd made up some excuse.

Sitting there in Hong Kong, struggling to even hold chopsticks properly, I couldn't help but think,

"I really should've gone with her."

Still...

I convinced myself I'd figure it out.

How hard could two sticks possibly be?

I was about to find out.

SUB ---------------------- Confidence Is a Dangerous Thing ----------------------

Now that I knew which pair of chopsticks was meant for serving and which pair was meant for eating, I confidently told myself,

"Easy."

"Just don't mix them up."

I looked at the red pair.

Then the black pair.

I repeated the colour code in my head a couple of times.

"Red for serving... black for eating."

Or was it...

"Black for serving... red for eating?"

You know where this is going.

Within the first five minutes...

I'd already forgotten.

I somehow managed to convince myself that black was for serving and red was for eating.

No one noticed.

Or at least...

I hoped no one noticed.

SUB ---------------------- Four Chopstick Coaches ----------------------

By this point, I had already become good friends with Boom, Japan (yes, that was still his nickname), and the two teachers from Thailand.

Unfortunately for them...

they had unknowingly signed up to become my personal chopstick coaches for the evening.

The moment they saw me holding the chopsticks...

they immediately knew I had absolutely no idea what I was doing.

The coaching session began.

"Hold it like a pen."

I tried.

Didn't work.

"Keep the gap between the chopsticks smaller."

I tried that too.

Still didn't work.

"Don't grip them so tightly."

Somehow...

that made it even worse.

Every piece of food had developed a personal grudge against me.

SUB ---------------------- Meat Wasn't the Problem ----------------------

To be fair...

meat wasn't actually that difficult.

It was big enough to grab.

Sometimes it slipped.

Sometimes I dropped it.

But eventually...

I'd get lucky.

The real villains of the evening...

were the vegetables.

They had been stir-fried in soy sauce.

They were shiny.

They were slippery.

And they absolutely refused to cooperate.

Every time I thought I had finally picked one up...

it would perform what can only be described as an Olympic-level escape and land right back onto the plate.

At this point, my table had stopped eating and had started watching.

Not because they were making fun of me.

They were genuinely trying to help.

Everyone had their own technique.

Everyone had their own advice.

None of it worked.

SUB ---------------------- The Worst Advice Ever Given ----------------------

Finally...

one of my Thai friends looked at me and said,

"Why don't you just use both chopsticks together and stab it?"

For one glorious second...

it sounded like a brilliant idea.

No balancing.

No slipping.

No frustration.

Just...

poke.

I looked at him.

He looked at me.

I did it.

The silence that followed...

told me everything.

Within seconds someone politely stopped me and explained that stabbing food with chopsticks was considered bad manners.

Apparently...

I'd discovered yet another unwritten rule.

At this point I had lost count of how many cultural mistakes I'd made during a single dinner.

And the evening...

wasn't even over yet.

SUB ---------------------- The Final Boss ----------------------

Just when I thought the worst was over...

the waiters arrived with the final dish.

Fried rice.

Now you guys might be wondering,

"Out of everything... fried rice?"

Exactly.

I thought the same thing.

The meat had already tested my patience.

The vegetables had completely destroyed my confidence.

Surely fried rice would be easy.

I couldn't have been more wrong.

The waiter placed a huge bowl of fried rice in the centre of the table.

Everyone served themselves.

Trying to fit in, I served myself exactly the way I'd seen in anime.

A nice little bowl...

with a perfect mound of rice sitting on top.

For a second, I even admired it.

"That actually looks pretty good."

SUB ---------------------- The Waiter Almost Had a Heart Attack ----------------------

Everything was perfectly normal.

Until...

I did something that made the waiter almost scream.

Without thinking...

I pushed my chopsticks straight into the bowl of rice so they stood upright.

The entire table froze.

The waiter immediately rushed towards me and said something in Cantonese that I obviously didn't understand.

For a second I genuinely thought,

"What have I done now?"

The Chinese delegate sitting next to me quickly leaned over and explained.

Apparently, sticking chopsticks upright into a bowl of rice resembles incense offered to the dead during funerals.

It's considered extremely unlucky.

So there I was...

on my very first authentic Chinese dinner...

accidentally inviting bad luck to the entire restaurant.

I apologised immediately.

Again.

At this point apologising had become part of the meal.

SUB ---------------------- Wait... Which Chopsticks Was I Using? ----------------------

As if that wasn't enough...

someone across the table suddenly looked at me and asked,

"Weren't you using the serving chopsticks to eat?"

My heart stopped.

I looked down.

Then I looked at both pairs of chopsticks.

Then back at everyone else.

Somewhere along the way...

I had completely mixed them up.

The colour code I'd spent five minutes memorising had officially disappeared from my brain.

I don't even remember who pointed it out.

I just remember wanting the table to swallow me whole.

SUB ---------------------- Too Much Anime ----------------------

Now if you've watched anime...

you'll know exactly what happens whenever a character makes a mistake.

They immediately stand up.

Bow.

And apologise.

So...

that's exactly what I did.

I stood up.

Bent forward.

And apologised as dramatically as I possibly could.

"Sumimasen!"

The entire table burst into laughter.

Someone quickly pulled me back into my chair and said,

"No, no... don't do that here."

Apparently making such a formal bow at the dinner table wasn't appropriate either.

Perfect.

Another cultural mistake added to the collection.

One of the teachers then smiled and said,

"Don't worry. I have a cousin who gets confused exactly like this."

For the first time that evening...

I realised I wasn't the only chaotic person in the world.

Everyone laughed.

I laughed.

The waiter eventually brought me another set of chopsticks.

It felt like my dinner had officially been reset.




















Day 2 — Macau

Theme: The conference brought us together. Macau gave us memories.



Day 2 — Macau: The City That Didn't Go According to Plan

The conference was finally over.

No presentations.

No rehearsing.

No checking my slides every five minutes.

For the first time since arriving in Hong Kong, I could simply enjoy the trip.

A group of us decided to spend the day exploring Macau before everyone headed back home.

By then, we'd stopped being "conference delegates."

We were just a bunch of friends wandering around another country together.

------------------------------Chapter 1 — My Casino Dream Lasted Exactly Thirty Seconds

If there was one place I wanted to visit in Macau, it was a casino.

Not because I wanted to gamble.

I'd simply never seen one from the inside.

Movies make casinos look magical—bright lights, poker tables, people dressed in suits, chandeliers hanging from the ceiling.

I just wanted to see what all the hype was about.

So naturally...

I was the first person standing in line.

Passport in hand.

Confidence at an all-time high.

The security guard looked at my passport.

Looked at me.

Looked back at my passport.

Then smiled.

"You're six months too early."

For a second, I genuinely thought he was joking.

He wasn't.

Turns out, the legal gambling age in Macau is 21.

I was still 20.

Six months.

I'd travelled all the way to Macau only to lose to six months.

Everyone else went inside.

I couldn't exactly say,

"Guys, nobody goes inside because I'm underage."

So I smiled, told them to enjoy themselves, and decided to explore the city on my own.

Looking back...

I'm glad things happened that way.

-----------------------Chapter 2 — The Best Part Was Outside

For the next couple of hours, Macau became my playground.

I had nowhere to be.

No timetable to follow.

No presentation waiting for me.

I just walked.

Every street looked expensive.

Designer stores lined almost every corner.

Ferraris and Lamborghinis casually drove past like they were ordinary cars.

Hotels looked less like hotels and more like palaces.

Coming from Bangalore, it almost felt surreal.

I probably walked much farther than I realised.

Sometimes I'd stop just to admire a building.

Sometimes I'd wander into a shopping street with no intention of buying anything.

Sometimes I'd simply sit for a few minutes and watch people walk by.

It's funny.

The thing I was most disappointed about that morning—missing out on the casino—ended up giving me some of my favourite memories from Macau.

---------------------------------------------------Chapter 3 — Mission Impossible

Of course...

I wasn't ready to give up that easily.

At some point, curiosity got the better of me.

"Surely there has to be another entrance."

That single thought turned into a full-fledged mission.

I walked around the casino.

Then around it again.

I mentally mapped every entrance, every exit, and every side road.

Each time I convinced myself I'd found a way in...

I was greeted by security.

There were two guards I still remember.

One Korean.

One Pakistani.

Those two somehow knew exactly where I was heading before I did.

Every entrance.

"No."

Another entrance.

"No."

Another entrance.

Still no.

After a while, I had to admit defeat.

The casino had officially won.

----------------------------------------------------------------Chapter 4 — Photobombing Boom

Eventually I found everyone again.

By then, the serious conference atmosphere had completely disappeared.

Everyone was taking pictures.

Boom, in particular, somehow looked like he belonged in every single photograph.

Naturally...

I decided that was my cue to make things less serious.

Every time someone pointed a camera at him...

I'd quietly appear somewhere in the background.

Sometimes making a weird face.

Sometimes pretending not to notice the camera.

Sometimes striking the most ridiculous pose I could think of.

The best part?

Boom never got annoyed.

He'd just laugh and let me continue ruining perfectly good photos.

Looking back at those pictures now...

I wouldn't change a single one.

-------------------------------------------------------------Chapter 5 — The Reveal I Never Saw Coming

The trip came to an end.

The Thai group flew back home the next morning.

Yuchen stayed back in Hong Kong for another day to meet an old friend from China.

A few days later, after everyone had reached home safely, I suddenly realised something.

I never asked Boom for his Instagram.

So I messaged Yuchen.

"Hey, do you have Boom's Instagram?"

She sent me his profile.

I opened it.

Scrolled once.

Scrolled again.

Then I just stared at my phone.

Boom...

the same Boom I'd met at a random bus stop.

The same Boom I'd had breakfast with.

The same Boom whose photos I'd spent an entire day photobombing...

...was a Vogue model.

Suddenly every single picture from the trip made sense.

The effortless smile.

The perfect posture.

The fact that he somehow looked good even in the photos I was trying my hardest to ruin.

The funniest part?

Nobody had mentioned it during the trip.

Not once.

And honestly...

I'm glad.

Because I got to know Boom as Boom.

Not as "the Vogue model."

Looking back...

I don't remember Boom as the Vogue model.

I remember the guy who smiled at me at a random bus stop.

The guy who laughed through breakfast.

The guy who got kicked out of our cab.

The guy whose pictures I kept photobombing.

Finding out he modelled for Vogue afterwards was hilarious.

But I'm glad I found out afterwards.

Because for those two days...

he was simply Boom.






